Below is a compact, self‑contained example that demonstrates the key ideas of **move semantics** in modern C++.  
It contains:

* A small “resource‑holding” type (`BigBuffer`) that owns a heap‑allocated array.
* A *move constructor* and *move assignment operator* that steal the array pointer and set the source to a safe “empty” state.
* A copy constructor/assignment for completeness (so we can see the difference).
* Functions that take and return the type by value to show how `std::move` is used to avoid unnecessary copies.

```cpp
/*********************************************************************
 *  Move Semantics Demo – C++17
 *
 *  Author: ChatGPT
 *  Date: 2026-10-09
 *
 *  This program shows:
 *    • What an rvalue reference is
 *    • How to write a move constructor / move assignment operator
 *    • When the compiler will use the move versions
 *    • How std::move forces a move
 *********************************************************************/

#include <iostream>
#include <utility>   // std::move, std::swap
#include <cstring>   // std::strlen, std::strcpy

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// A very simple "big" buffer that owns an array of chars
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
class BigBuffer {
public:
    // ---------- Constructors ---------------------------------------
    BigBuffer() noexcept
        : data_(nullptr), size_(0) {
        std::cout << "[BigBuffer] default ctor\n";
    }

    explicit BigBuffer(const char* s) {
        std::cout << "[BigBuffer] ctor from C‑string\n";
        size_ = std::strlen(s) + 1;          // +1 for '\0'
        data_ = new char[size_];
        std::strcpy(data_, s);
    }

    // ---------- Copy semantics -------------------------------------
    BigBuffer(const BigBuffer& other)
        : data_(nullptr), size_(0) {
        std::cout << "[BigBuffer] copy ctor\n";
        if (other.data_) {
            size_ = other.size_;
            data_ = new char[size_];
            std::memcpy(data_, other.data_, size_);
        }
    }

    BigBuffer& operator=(const BigBuffer& other) {
        std::cout << "[BigBuffer] copy assignment\n";
        if (this != &other) {
            BigBuffer tmp(other);       // copy-and-swap idiom
            swap(tmp);
        }
        return *this;
    }

    // ---------- Move semantics --------------------------------------
    BigBuffer(BigBuffer&& other) noexcept
        : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_) {
        std::cout << "[BigBuffer] move ctor\n";
        // Leave 'other' in a valid, empty state
        other.data_ = nullptr;
        other.size_ = 0;
    }

    BigBuffer& operator=(BigBuffer&& other) noexcept {
        std::cout << "[BigBuffer] move assignment\n";
        if (this != &other) {
            delete[] data_;            // free current resources
            data_ = other.data_;
            size_ = other.size_;
            other.data_ = nullptr;     // empty source
            other.size_ = 0;
        }
        return *this;
    }

    // ---------- Utility ---------------------------------------------
    void print() const {
        std::cout << "  BigBuffer: \"" << (data_ ? data_ : "(empty)") << "\"\n";
    }

    // ---------- Swap -------------------------------------------------
    void swap(BigBuffer& other) noexcept {
        std::swap(data_, other.data_);
        std::swap(size_, other.size_);
    }

    // ---------- Destructor ------------------------------------------
    ~BigBuffer() {
        std::cout << "[BigBuffer] destructor\n";
        delete[] data_;
    }

private:
    char*  data_;
    size_t size_;
};

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// A helper function that creates a BigBuffer and returns it by value
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
BigBuffer makeBuffer(const char* s) {
    BigBuffer tmp(s);
    std::cout << "  makeBuffer: constructed tmp\n";
    return tmp;                  // NRVO may elide the copy/move,
                                 // otherwise the move ctor is used
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// A function that takes ownership of a BigBuffer
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
void processBuffer(BigBuffer buf) {
    std::cout << "  processBuffer: received buffer\n";
    buf.print();
    // buffer will be destroyed at function exit
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// Main: exercising copy vs move
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
int main() {
    std::cout << "\n--- Create buffer1 with a string ---\n";
    BigBuffer buffer1("Hello, World!");

    std::cout << "\n--- Copy buffer2 from buffer1 ---\n";
    BigBuffer buffer2 = buffer1;   // copy ctor

    std::cout << "\n--- Move buffer3 from buffer1 ---\n";
    BigBuffer buffer3 = std::move(buffer1); // move ctor
    std::cout << "  After move, buffer1 is:\n";
    buffer1.print();   // should be empty

    std::cout << "\n--- Return a buffer from makeBuffer ---\n";
    BigBuffer buffer4 = makeBuffer("Temporary buffer");
    buffer4.print();

    std::cout << "\n--- Pass buffer4 to processBuffer ---\n";
    processBuffer(std::move(buffer4)); // force move into the function
    std::cout << "  After move into function, buffer4 is:\n";
    buffer4.print();   // should be empty

    std::cout << "\n--- End of main, destructors will run ---\n";
    return 0;
}
```

### What you’ll see when you run it

```
--- Create buffer1 with a string ---
[BigBuffer] ctor from C-string
  BigBuffer: "Hello, World!"

--- Copy buffer2 from buffer1 ---
[BigBuffer] copy ctor
  BigBuffer: "Hello, World!"

--- Move buffer3 from buffer1 ---
[BigBuffer] move ctor
  After move, buffer1 is:
  BigBuffer: "(empty)"

--- Return a buffer from makeBuffer ---
[BigBuffer] ctor from C‑string
  makeBuffer: constructed tmp
[BigBuffer] move ctor        // (if NRVO does NOT elide)
  BigBuffer: "Temporary buffer"

--- Pass buffer4 to processBuffer ---
[BigBuffer] move ctor        // (or copy ctor if NRVO elides)
  processBuffer: received buffer
  BigBuffer: "Temporary buffer"
  After move into function, buffer4 is:
  BigBuffer: "(empty)"

--- End of main, destructors will run ---
[BigBuffer] destructor
[BigBuffer] destructor
[BigBuffer] destructor
[BigBuffer] destructor
[BigBuffer] destructor
```

### Why this example matters

| Feature | What happens | Why it matters |
|---------|--------------|----------------|
| **`BigBuffer(BigBuffer&&)`** | The source’s pointer is *stolen*; source becomes empty | Avoids allocating/copying the large array |
| **`std::move(buffer)`** | Turns an lvalue into an rvalue reference | Allows the move constructor/assignment to be called |
| **`return tmp;`** | NRVO may eliminate the copy; otherwise move ctor used | Demonstrates how the compiler can avoid a move |
| **Copy vs. Move** | Copy ctor copies every byte; move ctor just reassigns pointer | Shows performance difference |
| **Destructors** | Only one destructor deletes the array (the one that actually owns it) | Prevents double‑free and leaks |

Feel free to compile with `-std=c++17` (or newer) and watch the console output to see which constructors/assignments are invoked. The program is intentionally verbose so you can follow the flow of ownership.
